[96m[researcher][0m [36m[thinking]I've been searching for specific information about the furan molecule's active space in CASSCF calculations considering only π orbitals. The arXiv/ChemRxiv search tools are not returning furan-specific results - they're mostly returning general CASSCF methodology papers and unrelated "space weather" papers.
[96m[researcher][0m 
[96m[researcher][0m Let me think about this from my chemistry knowledge. Furan (C₄H₄O) is a five-membered aromatic heterocyclic ring. It has:
[96m[researcher][0m 
[96m[researcher][0m - 5 atoms in the ring (4 C + 1 O)
[96m[researcher][0m - Each of the 4 carbon atoms contributes one p-orbital perpendicular to the ring plane
[96m[researcher][0m - The oxygen atom also contributes one p-orbital perpendicular to the ring plane
[96m[researcher][0m - So there are 5 π-type p-orbitals in total
[96m[researcher][0m 
[96m[researcher][0m For the electrons:
[96m[researcher][0m - Each C contributes 1 π electron = 4 electrons
[96m[researcher][0m - The O contributes 2 π electrons (the lone pair in the π system)
[96m[researcher][0m - Total = 6 π electrons
[96m[researcher][0m 
[96m[researcher][0m So the π-only CASSCF active space would be CAS(6,5) - 6 electrons in 5 orbitals.
[96m[researcher][0m 
[96m[researcher][0m This is a well-known result in computational chemistry. The 6 π electrons in 5 π orbitals follows Hückel's 4n+2 rule (with n=1), consistent with furan's aromatic character.
